From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 03:13:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nvme-pci: Look once more after the Apple ANS vector's last
 empty pass

On an iPad 6 (A10) a read waited for its 30 s timeout during a boot, and the
driver then found the completion posted: "I/O tag 32 (4020) QID 1 timeout,
completion polled". It is reproducible: 8.2 million direct 4 KiB reads alone,
one to sixteen at a time, gave no timeout, but the same reads while two
threads sent Identify and SMART log commands, as udisks2 does at its start,
stalled both queues for 30 s after 500 to 2500 interrupts, every time.

The handler services every CQ until a full pass finds nothing, but that pass
can come too early, because its CQ doorbell writes are posted. Until the
doorbell of one CQ reaches the controller, it takes the vector for busy and
signals no completion of the other. One that is posted after the pass has
looked at its CQ and before the doorbell arrives is missed, and it then keeps
the vector busy, so that nothing on either queue is signalled any more. A
completion on the same CQ as the doorbell is not lost, which fits the reads
alone never stalling.

Read a controller register after an empty pass and look once more. The read
can't pass the doorbell writes and its completion can't pass the controller's
earlier writes, so a pass that finds nothing after it is final.

With this change the same test ran 5 minutes, 877,000 interrupts and 166,000
admin commands beside the reads, without a timeout; the slowest read took
10 ms.

How the controller decides is inferred from this behaviour, not documented.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/nvme/host/pci.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
index 6708a44..01c0b90 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -1566,6 +1566,14 @@ static inline bool nvme_poll_cq(struct nvme_queue *nvmeq,
  * The actions of one IRQ never run concurrently, so this needs no more locking
  * than a single CQ. Threaded handlers can run concurrently and keep the
  * per-queue behaviour.
+ *
+ * The pass that finds nothing is only final once the controller has seen the
+ * CQ doorbells written before it, and everything the controller wrote before
+ * that has reached memory: until then a completion can be posted to a CQ the
+ * controller still takes for non-empty, with no MSI, or be on its way behind
+ * the MSI that announced it. A register read orders both (it can't pass the
+ * posted doorbell writes, and its completion can't pass the controller's
+ * posted writes), so read one after an empty pass and look once more.
  */
 static bool nvme_apple_ans_shared_vector(struct nvme_dev *dev)
 {
@@ -1585,19 +1593,31 @@ static bool nvme_apple_ans_vector_queue(struct nvme_queue *nvmeq, u16 vector)
 static bool nvme_apple_ans_irq_vector(struct nvme_dev *dev, u16 vector,
 				      struct io_comp_batch *iob)
 {
-	bool found = false, more;
+	bool found = false, flushed = false, more, enabled;
 	unsigned int i;
 
 	do {
 		more = false;
+		enabled = false;
 		for (i = 0; i < dev->ctrl.queue_count; i++) {
 			struct nvme_queue *nvmeq = &dev->queues[i];
 
-			if (nvme_apple_ans_vector_queue(nvmeq, vector) &&
-			    nvme_poll_cq(nvmeq, nvmeq->qid ? iob : NULL))
+			if (!nvme_apple_ans_vector_queue(nvmeq, vector))
+				continue;
+			enabled = true;
+			if (nvme_poll_cq(nvmeq, nvmeq->qid ? iob : NULL))
 				more = true;
 		}
 		found |= more;
+
+		if (more) {
+			flushed = false;
+		} else if (enabled && !flushed) {
+			/* With no queue enabled the link can be down */
+			readl(dev->bar + NVME_REG_CSTS);
+			flushed = true;
+			more = true;
+		}
 	} while (more);
 
 	return found;
